in exile for decades these palestinian  refugees have long lived on the margins  of society  they are among the most vulnerable and  poorest communities in lebanon their  situation has been made worse because  the economy is close to collapse  and now their lifeline faces a shortage  of funds  the financial crisis of unrwa has been  of an existential nature  and it's of an existential nature  because we cannot properly deliver our  services anymore at the time the needs  intercom have significantly increased  providing access to education to all the  palestinian refugees girls and boys in  the camp and in the country we are  providing assistance to more than 200  000 people we are providing primary  health care  lebanon's currency crash has hurt the  palestinians as well inflation led to  food prices increasing by tenfold  people are just buying the basics they  no longer buy in kilos but in grams  for many help from the u.n is vital  especially since the lebanese state  denies palestinians basic services  umrah provides education and medicine  but they should give us more help  because many people can afford to buy  bread  a recent decision by the lebanese  authorities to expand palestinian rights  in the labor market is welcomed here as  a way to deal with rising unemployment  but many tell us it's limited and what  is needed is to grant them basic rights  i can't register my truck under my name  i can't even get a license to drive it  there are minimum rights which  palestinians don't have we thank the  minister for the new decree but i doubt  it will resolve unemployment  palestinians have faced what rights  groups call discriminatory policies  since they first arrived in lebanon in  1948.  successive governments believe easing  restrictions and providing them  citizenship will encourage them to  settle here permanently that's why the  majority rely on the informal labor  market but opportunities are drying up  if we don't work we can't eat and there  isn't much work nowadays because of the  economic crisis  even before the economic collapse  lebanon's palestinians were struggling  it has since pushed more into poverty  the united nations says it now has to  find money so that the crisis won't have  what it calls a devastating humanitarian  impact senator al jazeera beirut
 

